{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213604",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we reduce our congruence to a congruence of the form $ax≡b,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4 & ≡34\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ 5x-4+4 & ≡34+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ 5x & ≡38\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ 5x & ≡2\\cdot 18+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ 5x & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=5$ and $n=18$, and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(5,18)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18).$ To find the inverse of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)$, we compute the exponents of $5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)$ until we get a result of $1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡25≡1\\cdot 18+7≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡7\\cdot 5≡35≡1\\cdot 18+17≡17 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ≡17\\cdot 5≡85≡4\\cdot 18+13≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ {5}^{5} & ≡13\\cdot 5≡65≡3\\cdot 18+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ {5}^{6} & ≡11\\cdot 5≡55≡3\\cdot 18+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $11\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18),$ which means that $11$ is the multiplicative inverse of $5$ (modulo $18$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $11,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ 11\\cdot 5x & ≡11\\cdot 2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ (11\\cdot 5)x & ≡22 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡4+1\\cdot 18 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18) \\\\ x & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18).$",
